# vibe-check — pre-launch compliance review

A compliance review for an app that was built fast. Run the scanner, reason
about what it cannot see, then produce one report: **PASS / FAIL / NEEDS
REVIEW** per check, each with `file:line` evidence and a fix.

## Non-negotiable rules

1. **Not legal advice, and say so.** End every report with the disclaimer at the
   bottom of this file, verbatim. Never tell the user they are "compliant",
   "legally safe", "covered" or "protected".
2. **Write conditionally.** Most of these laws apply only in certain
   situations: "if you have EU visitors…", "this may apply if…". COPPA applies
   **only** where the service is child-directed or you have actual knowledge of
   under-13 users — never state it as universal.
3. **Never invent a figure.** Use only the numbers in this file or in
   `SOURCES.md`. A missing DMCA agent is **not** a fine — the consequence is
   losing the §512 safe harbour. Never attach "$150,000" to it.
4. **Evidence or explicit absence.** Either `src/pay.ts:42`, or "no file in the
   project contains X".
5. **Report first, fix only when asked.**
6. **Never print a secret's value** — location and kind only.

## Step 1 — run the scanner

Zero-dependency Node, read-only, no network:

```bash
node .cursor/vibe-check/scan.mjs . --json
```

The installer puts it there. If that path does not exist, find it:

```bash
find . ~/.vibe-check ~/.claude/skills -name scan.mjs -path '*vibe-check*' 2>/dev/null | head -1
```

Still nothing? Say so, and do the review by hand with the grep patterns below.
Do not silently skip checks — an unchecked item is never a PASS.

Flags: `--json` to parse, `--md` for a ready-made report, `--only <ids>`,
`--max-evidence 30`, `--list`. Exit code is `1` when any check FAILs.

## Step 2 — the 16 checks

Confirm each scanner FAIL by opening the cited line (dead code and doc examples
are false positives), and resolve NEEDS REVIEW items by reading the code where
you can.

| Check | FAIL when | The rule, conditionally stated |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| `google-fonts` | `fonts.googleapis.com` / `fonts.gstatic.com` in source | Sends every EU visitor's IP to Google pre-consent. LG München I, 20 Jan 2022, **3 O 17493/20**, awarded **€100**. Fix: self-host the `.woff2` files. `next/font/google` self-hosts at build time and is fine. |
| `tracking-before-consent` | analytics / pixels / session replay with no consent gate | EU: ePrivacy **Art. 5(3)** needs prior consent for non-essential tags. California: CIPA claims; **Cal. Penal Code §637.2** allows **$5,000 per violation** or 3× actual. Note SB 690 (effective 1 Jan 2027) makes §638.51 pen-register claims AG-only for websites; §631/§632 claims survive. |
| `cookie-consent-banner` | trackers present, no consent library or gate | Reject must be as easy as Accept, nothing pre-ticked, granular, withdrawable. |
| `secrets-exposed` | live keys in client code, bundle, or a committed `.env` | Rotate first, then move server-side. Anything behind `NEXT_PUBLIC_`/`VITE_`/`REACT_APP_` is published. A Firebase `AIza…` web key is public by design — not a leak. |
| `open-database` | RLS disabled, `using (true)`, or `allow read, write: if true` | The anon key is in the browser. Deny by default, scope every policy to `auth.uid()`. Verify in the hosted dashboard — policies are not in the repo. |
| `privacy-policy-terms` | either document missing | CalOPPA **Cal. B&P §22575** (30-day cure after notice; up to **$2,500** per violation via the UCL). GDPR **Arts. 13–14** if EU users. The policy must name the processors the code actually uses. |
| `ccpa-do-not-sell` | ad pixels present, no opt-out link | CCPA/CPRA **Cal. Civ. Code §1798.135**. Applies only above a threshold (**>$26.625M** revenue, **100,000+** CA consumers/households, or **≥50%** of revenue from selling PI) **and** if you sell or share — ad pixels count as sharing. Honour `navigator.globalPrivacyControl`. Fines up to **$2,663** per violation / **$7,988** intentional. |
| `dmca-agent` | user uploads/UGC with no DMCA agent or takedown process | **17 U.S.C. §512(c)(2)** — designation is a threshold condition for the safe harbour. Register at `dmca.copyright.gov`, **$6**. No agent = no safe harbour, **not** a fine. |
| `coppa-age-gate` | child-directed signals with no age collection | **15 U.S.C. §§6501–6506**; **16 C.F.R. Part 312**. Amended rule published **22 Apr 2025**, full compliance by **22 Apr 2026**; civil penalty up to **$53,088** per violation. Applies only if child-directed or on actual knowledge. Collect date of birth, not "are you 13?". |
| `can-spam` | email sending with no unsubscribe or no postal address | **15 U.S.C. §7704**; **16 C.F.R. Part 316**. Up to **$53,088 per email** (FTC figure in force since 17 Jan 2025, unchanged for 2026). Purely transactional mail is exempt from the opt-out duty. EU/UK generally needs consent first. |
| `auto-renew-terms` | recurring billing with no renewal disclosure or no online cancel | California ARL **B&P §17600 et seq.**, amended by **AB 2863** for contracts from **1 Jul 2025**; **§17603** deems goods sent without affirmative consent an **unconditional gift**. Federal ROSCA still applies — the FTC click-to-cancel rule was vacated July 2025 and is being re-proposed. |
| `accessibility-basics` | missing `alt`, unlabelled inputs, no `lang`, positive `tabindex`, click-on-div | Target **WCAG 2.2 AA**. ADA Title III litigation is the US private-sector exposure. EU **European Accessibility Act** has applied to consumer e-commerce since **28 Jun 2025** (EN 301 549 → WCAG 2.1 AA); **micro-enterprises** (<10 staff, ≤€2M) are exempt from the service duties. Contrast and keyboard need a browser. |
| `ai-disclosure` | user-facing AI chat with no disclosure text | EU AI Act **Art. 50** (Reg. 2024/1689) applies from **2 Aug 2026** and was **not** delayed by the Digital Omnibus; Art. 50(2) marking had a grace period to **2 Dec 2026** for pre-existing systems. California B.O.T. Act **B&P §17941** — disclosure is the safe harbour; up to **$2,500** per violation via the UCL. |
| `refund-policy` | payments with no refund policy | FTC Act §5 for undisclosed terms. EU **CRD 2011/83/EU** gives a **14-day** withdrawal right; for instant digital delivery you need express consent to begin **plus** acknowledgement that the right is lost. |
| `sales-tax-vat` | advisory note only — never fails | EU: VAT due in the customer's state via OSS; the **€10,000** threshold is for **EU-established** sellers only, so a non-EU seller registers from the first sale. US: economic nexus after **Wayfair** (2018), varying by state. |
| `third-party-licences` | no `LICENSE`, fonts with no licence file, restrictive deps | Copyleft (GPL/AGPL/SSPL) can reach your source — AGPL in a hosted app especially. Desktop font EULAs usually forbid `@font-face`. Keep a `CREDITS.md`. |

## Grep patterns, if the scanner is unavailable

```bash
grep -rn "fonts.googleapis.com\|fonts.gstatic.com" --include="*.html" --include="*.css" --include="*.ts*" --include="*.js*" .
grep -rn "googletagmanager\|gtag(\|fbq(\|hotjar\|clarity.ms\|fullstory\|logrocket\|posthog\|mixpanel" --include="*.ts*" --include="*.js*" --include="*.html" .
grep -rn "sk_live_\|sk-ant-api\|sk-proj-\|AKIA\|ghp_\|service_role\|-----BEGIN .*PRIVATE KEY" .
grep -rn "disable row level security\|using (true)\|allow read, write: if true\|\"\.read\": true" .
grep -rn "privacy\|terms\|unsubscribe\|refund\|DMCA\|do not sell" --include="*.ts*" --include="*.js*" --include="*.html" --include="*.md" .
grep -rn "<img" --include="*.html" --include="*.tsx" --include="*.jsx" . | grep -v "alt="
```

## Step 3 — the report

Order worst-first. For each failure: **what**, **where** (`file:line`), **why it
may matter** (with the citation and its date), and a **fix** the user can paste
or do in ten minutes. Then NEEDS REVIEW items, each with the specific question
the user must answer. Then PASSes, one line each. Then a **Not checked** section
— never let an unchecked item appear as a PASS.

Mark as NEEDS REVIEW anything that needs a browser (colour contrast, keyboard
traps, whether tags really wait for consent), a hosted dashboard (Supabase or
Firebase rules), or a fact only the user knows (revenue thresholds, markets,
whether the DMCA agent is actually registered).

If the repo does not tell you, ask all four scope questions at once: EU/UK
visitors? California visitors? User uploads? Recurring charges?

Close by offering to fix the failures. Do not start fixing.

